			<div class="content" id="post-content-4845307">
									

<p>Hey there, thanks for the crit!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Here&nbsp;are&nbsp;my&nbsp;Responses&nbsp;+</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close&nbsp;-</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>This feels like although I think a pattern screamer does hate all existence… they don't seem like murderous beings, so much as wanting to erase existence as a concept?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a completely valid point here, but these are special circumstances. I'd liken it to this situation: you've been locked in a basement, and a person begins torturing you. Do you hate the person who locked you in the basement or the person torturing you more? I, personally, would hate the torturer more, as while being locked in a basement is bad, the torturer makes it much worse. Even though I'd hate the person who locked me in the basement for enabling the torture, I'd hate the torturer more for, well, torturing me.</p>
<p>In this case, our world is the 'basement' for this entity; it hates being here. However, the people actively perceiving it (and their brains 'filling in the gap' of the entity, <em>trying</em> to make it real) are the torturer/s, and thus it hates them more. It's not murderous per se, more 'defensive' (as I said in my response to cybersqyd, it's lashing out). And in this case, lashing out is all it can do. It can't get rid of the basement, so it gets rid of the torturers.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>ALSO, I don't understand this concept of someone becoming a pattern screamer… which doesn't seem to fit at all. These things are unknowable, pure hatred machines that exist outside of reality.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, valid point. I, uh, didn't really explain what's happening here, so I'll do that now. In my headcanon for SCP-3930, when people enter the void they become a pattern screamer, quickly losing their minds as they come to terms with the fact that they don't exist, and <em>can't</em>, but are being made to exist. This entity essentially acts as a smaller version of the void, in that it drags people into itself so that they cease to exist (and thus, as part of my headcanon, become a pattern screamer). It also kind of warps everything around it slightly (space trying to fill in what should be a gap, but it isn't). Hope this makes sense!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[1]I just don't think this will add much to the concept. [2]And in terms of having an enjoyable horror story, I don't think this is innovative enough. [3]Again, I don't think that Pattern Screamers fits this sorta story format at all (horror comes out of something and hunts down people in a facility).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Let's sort this out in sections:<br />
[1] - I disagree with you slightly here. Mostly because I myself am curious about the incident mentioned in the SCP-3930 article (also found in my summary: &quot;Given enough time, and with enough of us trying to look into this void, something crawled out of it.&quot;), and this could serve as an explanation for a presumably terrifying incident that isn't elaborated on whatsoever. However, this is only assuming I could execute this successfully, which may happen, but might not.<br />
[2] - I mean, I can't really argue with you here. More of a matter of taste really.<br />
[3] - To reiterate, this is building off of a serious incident that is only vaguely mentioned in the source article. While this tale mightn't be a good fit for the Pattern Screamers ordinarily, due to extraordinary circumstances I believe it does fit as an elaboration on an incident.</p>
<p>Basically, this Tale aims to fill in a gap in the story of SCP-3930, whilst still maintaining the whole 'unfathomable unreality' feel that is so present within it.</p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Anyway, thanks again for the crit, hope this helps with your concerns!
							</div>

								
					
